import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/PushCompose
import ../../../Demos/PushStates
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Demo
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The push package, a plugin: the package's example app file by file, `<PushButton>` and its
## states, a notification and its options (live: the package's functions in the browser), sending,
## how Web Push goes, the modules, safety, and what works today and what waits on a proposal.
## Static but for the notification demo: this site is files, and a push needs a server.
meta = {
	title: "Push",
	description: "Web Push notifications for Roc Solid apps, as a plugin: a button that subscribes, a table that keeps each browser's subscription, and jobs that encrypt, sign and send.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "example", label: "A first app" },
		{ id: "button", label: "<PushButton>" },
		{ id: "notification", label: "A notification" },
		{ id: "sending", label: "Sending" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "safety", label: "Safety" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/push</code>]}
				title="Notifications that arrive with every tab closed"
			>
				Web Push lets a server notify a browser that agreed to it, even with none of the site's tabs open.
				This plugin has every part: a button that asks and subscribes, a table of each browser's
				subscription, and a job that sends.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Use it for news a user wants while away from your site: a reply to their question, a finished
				export, a shipped order.
			</p>

			<Heading id="example">A first app</Heading>
			<p>
				Each file below is from <code>packages/push/example</code>: a page with the subscribe button and a
				button that notifies everyone. It can't run on this site, which is static files: Web Push needs a
				server.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>1. Keys.</strong> Web Push identifies your server by a key pair, the VAPID keys. Under
				<code>rocx dev</code> you need none to start: the server makes a pair for the run and logs the two
				settings that keep it:
			</p>
			<Code title="The server's log" source={"WARN  push: no VAPID keys, so this run made a pair, which the next start replaces (a browser subscribed with it then subscribes again); to keep it, put these in .env, a line each: VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=BKx… VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=3q…"} plain numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>rocx dev</code> restarts the server at each change to its code, and a new pair means each
				browser's button shows off until clicked again. To keep the pair, copy the two settings into
				<code>.env</code>, which <code>rocx dev</code> reads as it starts. Without <code>VAPID_SUBJECT</code>,
				the run tells push services <code>mailto:dev@example.com</code>, and logs that.
			</p>
			<p>
				A deployed server needs all three in its environment: <code>VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY</code>,
				<code>VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY</code> and <code>VAPID_SUBJECT</code> (<code>mailto:</code> and an address, or
				an <code>https:</code> URL). Give it its own pair: under <code>rocx dev</code>,
				<code>/__rocx/push/keys</code> answers a new one each time, as the two lines to set.
			</p>
			<Code source={"curl -s localhost:8080/__rocx/push/keys\nVAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=BNc…\nVAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=Hw…"} plain numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A browser can only be sent to with the pair it subscribed with, so keep the keys once you have users.
				Without <code>--dev</code>, a missing key or subject, or two keys that aren't a pair, stop the server
				at start, and it says why and how to make a pair.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>2. The plugin.</strong> Name the package in the app's header and list its plugin, with the
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>jobs</a> plugin, which runs the job that sends:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.push_example_main.file} source={Sources.push_example_main.rocx} />
			<p>The plugin brings:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					its table, <code>push_subscriptions</code>, in the app's database: Postgres or SQLite, and SQLite's
					<code>data/app.db</code> when the program names none, as here;
				</li>
				<li>its job, <code>push.deliver</code>, which the jobs plugin's workers run;</li>
				<li>
					the service worker at <code>/push-sw.js</code> and a script every page loads, both served before
					the app's middleware, so a guard never blocks them;
				</li>
				<li>under <code>--dev</code> only, <code>GET /__rocx/push/keys</code>.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Its three endpoints are your routes, each a file calling the package's handler behind its folder's
				middleware: <code>GET /push/key</code> (the public key), <code>POST /push/subscribe</code> and
				<code>POST /push/unsubscribe</code>, where <code>{"<PushButton api=\"/push\">"}</code> looks. Put them
				elsewhere and give the button that path.
			</p>
			<Code title="routes/push/subscribe/+server.roc" source={"import push.Push\n\n# Whose the subscription is: \"\" for a visitor, or the app's id for the user this folder's middleware\n# handed on (`context.member`); then Push.notify! can send to that user's browsers.\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| Push.subscribe_route!(request, \"\")"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Push.key_route!(request)"}</code> and <code>{"Push.unsubscribe_route!(request, owner)"}</code>
				answer the other two the same way.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>3. The page.</strong> The button, and a handler that asks an endpoint to notify everyone:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.push_example_page.file} source={Sources.push_example_page.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.push_example_announce.file} source={Sources.push_example_announce.rocx} />

			<Heading id="button">{"<PushButton>"}</Heading>
			<p>
				A button that turns this browser's notifications on and off. A click calls the plugin's script
				(<code>static/push.js</code>) with <a href={"${Paths.components}#handlers"}><code>Browser.call!</code></a>,
				which asks the browser's permission, registers the service worker, subscribes with the server's
				public key and posts the subscription to the server. Turning off unsubscribes and tells the server.
				Browsers show the permission prompt only during a click, key press or tap, so subscribing starts
				from a button.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>api : Str</code></td>
							<td>
								where your routes for push are: with <code>"/push"</code>, <code>GET /push/key</code>,
								<code>POST /push/subscribe</code> and <code>POST /push/unsubscribe</code>
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>Browser.call!</code> can't return an answer, so the script reports with a <code>pushstate</code>
				event, which every <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> on the page hears: how a click went, and, when the
				page loads, where this browser stands. The button shows each report as a <code>PushState</code>:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>The script says</th><th>The button shows</th></tr></thead>
					<PushStates />
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>aria-pressed</code> says whether notifications are on, and a screen reader reads the note when
				it changes (<code>aria-live</code>). On an iPhone or iPad, a site gets Web Push only once it is added
				to the Home Screen and opened from there.
			</p>
			<details>
				<summary>The button: <code>{Sources.push_button.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.push_button.file} source={Sources.push_button.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="notification">A notification</Heading>
			<p>
				A notification is a title, a body, the page a click opens (<code>url</code>) and a <code>tag</code>.
				Its options tell the push service how to treat the message. Change them below to see what a server
				would send, worked out by the package's own functions in your browser.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo>
			<div data-demo="push-compose"><PushCompose /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<Note>
				The request has two more headers: <code>Authorization</code> (the VAPID token, <code>vapid t=…, k=…</code>)
				and <code>Content-Encoding: aes128gcm</code>. The data is encrypted: the push service sees only bytes.
			</Note>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>title : Str</code></td><td>the notification's first line; empty: the site's host name</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>body : Str</code></td><td>the text under it</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>url : Str</code></td><td>the page a click opens, on this site only; empty: the front page</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>tag : Str</code></td><td>a newer notification with the same tag replaces this one, rather than piling up</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>icon : Str</code></td><td>an image's URL</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>{"Notification.new(title, body)"}</code> makes one with the other fields empty, and
				<code>{"{ ..Notification.new(title, body), url: \"/orders/17\" }"}</code> sets more. Empty fields are
				left out of the JSON.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>ttl : U64</code></td>
							<td>
								how many seconds the push service keeps the message for a browser that is offline. 0 means now
								or never; more than four weeks is sent as four weeks.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>urgency : [VeryLow, Low, Normal, High]</code></td>
							<td>how soon it must arrive. A phone on battery may hold the low ones back.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>topic : Str</code></td>
							<td>
								a newer message with the same topic replaces one still waiting, so only the latest status
								arrives. At most 32 characters, of letters, digits, <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>.
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p><code>Options.defaults</code> is a day, <code>Normal</code>, and no topic.</p>
			<details>
				<summary>This demo: <code>{Sources.push_compose.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.push_compose.file} source={Sources.push_compose.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="sending">Sending</Heading>
			<p>
				On the server, a job or endpoint calls <code>Push.notify!</code> (one user's browsers) or
				<code>Push.broadcast!</code> (every browser). Each checks the notification and options, queues a
				<code>push.deliver</code> job for each subscription, and answers how many it queued. Inside a
				transaction, the jobs are queued only if it commits.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.push_send.file} source={Sources.push_send.rocx} />
			<p>A jobs worker sends each message; the push service's answer decides what happens next:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>The push service answers</th><th>The job</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>201, or any 2xx</td><td>is done, and notes when it sent</td></tr>
						<tr><td>404 or 410</td><td>deletes the subscription: the browser unsubscribed, or it expired</td></tr>
						<tr><td>408, 429 or 5xx, with <code>Retry-After</code></td><td>sends it again at that time, five times at most, while the message's TTL lasts</td></tr>
						<tr><td>408, 429 or 5xx without it, or no answer</td><td>fails the attempt; the worker tries again, waiting longer each time (five attempts)</td></tr>
						<tr><td>400, 401, 403, 413, or anything else</td><td>logs it and notes it on the subscription; sending again wouldn't help</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A retry sends the TTL that is left, so a message never outlives it. A 403 doesn't delete the
				subscription: push services answer 403 to wrong VAPID keys, and a mistake in the server's settings
				shouldn't delete every subscription.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How it works</Heading>
			<ol>
				<li>
					A click on <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> asks the browser's permission.
				</li>
				<li>
					The browser subscribes at its push service (Google's for Chrome, Mozilla's for Firefox, Apple's
					for Safari, Microsoft's for Edge) with the server's public key. It gets a subscription: a URL at
					that service, and two keys.
				</li>
				<li>The page posts the subscription to the app, which keeps a row for each browser.</li>
				<li>Later, the server calls <code>Push.notify!</code>, which queues a job for each of the user's subscriptions.</li>
				<li>
					The job encrypts the notification for that one browser (RFC 8291), signs a token that says which
					server sent it (VAPID, RFC 8292), and posts both to the subscription's URL (RFC 8030). The
					elliptic-curve work and the AES are the server platform's
					(<a href={"${Paths.platform_crypto}#p256"}><code>P256</code> and <code>AesGcm</code></a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					The push service wakes the browser. The package's service worker shows the notification, and a
					click on it opens the page it names.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<p>
				<strong>Plain Roc</strong>, on no platform: the formats, the key derivation, the token, and what
				each answer means. The browser's half of an app can use them too (<code>browser.roc</code> lists
				them). <code>roc test packages/push/main.roc</code> checks them against the RFCs' own examples. Every
				function is in <code>packages/push/README.md</code>.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Subscription</code></td><td>a browser's subscription: <code>parse</code> (the page's JSON), <code>from_body</code> (an endpoint's body), <code>from_text</code> and <code>to_text</code> (a table row), <code>to_json</code>; <code>known_service</code>: is it at Chrome's, Firefox's, Safari's or Edge's push service?</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Notification</code></td><td>what the service worker shows: <code>new</code>, <code>to_json</code>, <code>data</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Options</code></td><td>how a push service treats a message: <code>defaults</code>, <code>check</code> (the topic), <code>headers</code>, <code>remaining</code> (the TTL left for a retry)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Delivery</code></td><td><code>request</code> (the POST to a subscription's URL); <code>outcome</code> (what an answer means: <code>Delivered</code>, <code>Gone</code>, <code>RetryAfter(ms)</code>, <code>Retry(why)</code>, <code>Refused(why)</code>), <code>retry_after</code>, <code>http_date</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Vapid</code></td><td>the server's token, RFC 8292: <code>Keys</code> and <code>from_text</code> (from the environment), <code>audience</code>, <code>claims</code> (valid 12 hours), <code>signing_input</code>, <code>token</code>, <code>authorization</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>WebPush</code></td><td>the message's encryption, RFC 8291: <code>prepare</code> (everything before the AES), <code>body</code>, <code>max_data</code> (3993 bytes)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Ece</code></td><td>the <code>aes128gcm</code> content coding, RFC 8188: <code>header</code>, <code>parse_header</code>, <code>pad</code>, <code>unpad</code>, <code>keys</code>, <code>nonce</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>PushState</code></td><td>where a browser stands, as the page's script reports it: what <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> shows</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				HMAC-SHA-256, HKDF-SHA-256 and base64url come from <a href={Paths.ecosystem_crypto}>the crypto
				package</a>, which push names in its own header: an app names <code>push</code> alone.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>The plugin</strong>, <code>{"Push.plugin({})"}</code>, on the server platform, which the
				package names in its header (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugins</a>): its table and
				its migrations (<code>migrations/</code>, for Postgres and for SQLite), the job that sends, and the
				handlers the app's routes call. Each function's <code>owner</code> is the app's id for a user, as
				text, or <code>""</code> for a visitor.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Push</code></td><td><code>{"plugin({})"}</code>, the plugin, with no options; <code>key_route!</code>, <code>subscribe_route!</code> and <code>unsubscribe_route!</code>, the handlers the app's route files call; <code>subscribe!(body, owner)</code> and <code>unsubscribe!(endpoint, owner)</code>, which those call; <code>notify!</code> and <code>broadcast!</code>; <code>public_key!</code> and <code>keys!</code> (from the environment, or under <code>--dev</code> without keys the pair the run made); <code>deliver</code>, the job; <code>send!</code>, one attempt</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>PushStore</code></td><td>the <code>push_subscriptions</code> table: <code>save!</code> (a row for each endpoint), <code>remove!</code>, <code>remove_id!</code>, <code>find!</code>, <code>of_owner!</code>, <code>everyone!</code>, and <code>delivered!</code> and <code>refused!</code>, which the job calls</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<strong>The browser's part</strong>: <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code>, in <code>components/</code>, which
				<code>rocx build</code> copies into the app, and these files in <code>static/</code>, which the
				plugin serves:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>push.js</code></td><td>the page's script, at <code>/rocx/plugins/push/push.js</code>, on every page: the <code>push.subscribe</code> and <code>push.unsubscribe</code> effects the button calls. Once a visit, it sends the server the browser's subscription again, in case it lost it. On a site served under a base path (<code>/rocx/</code>), every URL it uses is under that path.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>push-sw.js</code></td><td>the service worker, at the site's root (<code>/push-sw.js</code>), so its scope is the whole site. It shows a message's notification; a click focuses a tab already at its URL, or opens one. When the push service replaces a subscription, it tells the server.</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="safety">Safety</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					The server posts to whatever URL a subscription names, so <code>Push.subscribe!</code> accepts only
					<code>https:</code> URLs at the browsers' own push services. Under <code>--dev</code> (as
					<code>rocx dev</code> starts it), it also accepts an <code>http:</code> URL on this machine, for a
					stand-in push service.
				</li>
				<li>
					The private key never leaves the server; the page gets only the public key. Only under
					<code>--dev</code>, on your machine, is a pair logged or answered; a deployed server never makes one.
				</li>
				<li>A click on a notification opens only this site's pages.</li>
				<li>Messages are encrypted for the browser: the push service sees only bytes.</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>Tested today:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					All the plain Roc, by <code>expect</code>s against the RFCs' own examples. The server platform's
					tests check its AES against RFC 8291's and RFC 8188's examples, and its ECDH, ES256 and key pairs
					against Node's crypto.
				</li>
				<li>
					The plugin, in the example app on SQLite, and on Postgres when one is available. It saves and
					forgets subscriptions, and refuses one at an unknown service or with a key off the curve. A
					broadcast goes to a push service the test runs, which plays the browsers too: it checks each
					request's VAPID token and signature, decrypts each message as a browser would, and answers so that
					a gone subscription is deleted and a refusal is noted on its row. The key checks at start, the dev
					pair and <code>/__rocx/push/keys</code> are tested too.
				</li>
				<li>
					The button, script and service worker, in Chromium behind a guard that refuses every script the
					app would serve: the plugin's files still come through, the worker's scope is the whole site, and
					the button subscribes.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				No test sends to a real push service (Google's, Mozilla's, Apple's or Microsoft's). They accept what
				the RFCs say, which the tests check; a real browser's first notification is the last check.
			</p>
			<p>Not yet (each has a proposal in <code>packages/push/PROPOSALS.md</code>):</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Jobs that give up, or come back at a set time.</strong> Today a refused message ends its job
					as if it had been sent, and a <code>Retry-After</code> queues a copy of the job for that time.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Browser.call!</code> with an answer.</strong> Until a call can answer, the script tells
					the button how it went with a DOM event (<code>pushstate</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A limit: a message holds 3993 bytes of data, the notification's JSON. <code>Push.notify!</code>
				refuses a longer one.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
